        "title": "chart-storytelling — three visual sequences",
        "instructions": "# Editorial motion with honest geometry\n\nRows 1–2 are moments from the original Field Notes data study; row 3 is a software\nworkflow illustration. All sample figures and names are illustrative. Never copy\nthem into a user's chart. Borrow hierarchy, timing and grouping, not their data.\n\n1. **Orient, encode, emphasize:** establish a title and scale, reveal marks from\n   a common baseline, then move attention to a supported comparison. Keep exact\n   values readable after the motion settles. One meaningful highlight is stronger\n   than making every bar a different neon color.\n2. **Explain relationships:** introduce entities, connect them, then emphasize a\n   path. This works for flow/process diagrams when the user supplied that meaning;\n   decorative connectivity must not invent causation between numerical categories.\n3. **Group and reorganize:** objects can enter as a collection, sort into groups,\n   and resolve into a structured view. Preserve identities and values throughout.\n   For dense data, use successive panels with a stable scale and an explicit scope.\n\n## Design the explanatory action\n\nAsk what motion makes easier to understand. Bars can grow together from zero,\nthen a bracket or subtle focus region can explain the largest supported difference.\nA line can draw through its actual data points in order, with markers and labels\narriving at the drawing front. A process can move one token through named stages.\nThe structure should still be clear if the decorative background is hidden.\n\nDo not scale a complete line about its center while its markers stay fixed: the\nintermediate geometry disconnects from the data. Use coherent segment reveals,\nclip/wipe mechanisms supported by the installed grammar, or synchronized points.\nKeep the numeric domain visible when a line's baseline is not zero. Never use\nelastic magnitude overshoot, 3D perspective or a camera tilt to exaggerate data.\n\nGive the chart an editorial setting: strong type hierarchy, an intentional light\nor dark surface, one accent and generous plot margins. Bold art direction belongs\naround truthful marks, not in distorted numeric encoding. A changing title alone\ndoes not make the chart animated. Final values need a substantial reading hold.\n\nSources: Dojo Video Engine MIT studies `data-story` and `software`.\n",
