        "id": "motion-atlas",
        "title": "festival-motion — three visual sequences",
        "instructions": "# Read this board as choreography\n\nRows are three different design directions; columns are successive moments of\neach original 12-second film. Adapt the relationships to the current duration,\nformat and occasion. These are visual studies, not templates or project assets.\n\n1. **Type as stage:** monumental lettering and diagonal color fields make the\n   whole frame participate. One word/number becomes a stage; the next beat changes\n   the composition before the identity settles. Borrow the scale contrast and\n   directional handoff, not the sample words or checkerboard everywhere.\n2. **Sculptural celebration:** a dimensional centerpiece, orbit and typography\n   share one visual world. Depth, lighting and foreground/background separation\n   do more than a larger particle count. For a six-second birthday, a wrapped\n   object could open into the age, with ribbons carrying the reveal into the name.\n3. **Organic reveal:** overlapping vector forms unfurl in waves, then open a calm\n   reading area. Build two or three related shapes with stagger and layered depth;\n   quantity is not the idea. A formal anniversary could reveal a precise emblem\n   through measured arcs rather than borrowing birthday balloons.\n\nChoose the direction because it fits the brief. A concise concept might be\n“paper strips unfold into a birthday stage, then sweep aside to reveal Maya.”\nDescribe the visual action before choosing a palette. A single large disc, age,\nname and scattered confetti is only a starting card, not a finished motion story.\n\n## Translate the reference into authored beats\n\nFor a roughly six-second graphic, a useful starting rhythm is: anticipation in\nthe first half-second; a distinctive transformation over the next two seconds;\nthe name/message becomes the focus; then at least 1.5 seconds of clear final hold.\nUse that rhythm as a design aid, not a mandatory duration formula. Make at least\ntwo adjacent beats visibly different in silhouette, arrangement or framing;\nchanging opacity alone does not create a new composition.\n\nUse native groups/shapes for paper, masks and type, SVG import for a bespoke motif,\nor the installed Three scene grammar for a genuinely dimensional centerpiece.\nKeep exact names and ages as whole editable text nodes. Never invent a logo, date,\nvenue or cultural symbol from a person's name. The board's copy is illustrative.\n\nSources: Dojo Video Engine MIT studies `kinetic`, `festival`, `bloom`.\nExact frame numbers and source video hashes are in the sibling provenance.json.\n",
